  "author": "",
  "attributionNote": "The hymns are anonymous. The selection, the introduction and the grammatical and explanatory notes are Arthur Anthony Macdonell's, written for students beginning Sanskrit and Vedic poetry.",
  "description": "Macdonell's reader introduces the Rigveda through thirty hymns chosen to illustrate its gods, its style and its thought, headed by the deities addressed — Agni, Indra, the Maruts, Varuna, Ushas, the funeral hymns, the gambler and the hymn of creation. An introductory essay on age, arrangement, language, metre and religion, and a glossary, make the volume a compact course in Vedic poetry rather than a complete translation.",
  "historicalContext": "Arthur Anthony Macdonell (1854–1930) was Boden Professor of Sanskrit at Oxford and one of the editors of the Vedic index. His reader, first published in 1917, trained several generations of Sanskritists and is still used for the study of Vedic grammar and metre.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete as published: the introduction, thirty selected hymns with notes and the glossary, streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time. The archive serves the reader as one page, so the library splits it at the printed headings — the introduction's ten sections and each hymn — and serves one heading per chapter. This is a selection from the Rigveda, not the whole collection.",
